#: manual/api/logic.md:1
msgid "logic API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:5
msgid "The `logic` package (`Luna-Flow/QED/logic`) is the checked helper layer over the kernel. It defines the propositional connectives as kernel definitions, builds and recognises connective terms, derives the propositional rules from the primitive ones, and keeps the catalog of theorem names that `exact` and `apply` understand. It depends only on `kernel` and has no authority of its own: every theorem it returns was produced by kernel rules, so a bug here can make a proof fail but cannot make a false theorem."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:7
msgid "All functions are pure. Theorem-producing functions return `Result[@kernel.Thm, @kernel.LogicError]`; helpers that may find nothing return an `Option`. The logic behind the definitions is explained in the [logic design](../design/logic.md) and a walk-through is in the [logic tutorial](../tutorial/logic.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:9
msgid ""
"In the formulas on this page, $\\top$ is the constant `T`, $\\bot$ the constant `F`, and $p \\wedge q$, $p \\Rightarrow q$, $\\n"
"eg p$, $p \\vee q$ the connective terms built by `prop_mk_and`, `prop_mk_imp`, `prop_mk_not` and `prop_mk_or`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:22
msgid "The examples on this page are blackbox tests. They refer to this package as `@logic` and also use `@kernel`, so they import both packages."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:24
msgid "Prelude"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:28
msgid "`PropPrelude` names the constants of the propositional prelude and lists its rule names."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:42
msgid "Most functions of this package take a `PropPrelude` to know which constants to look for in the state."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:46
msgid "`PropRule` is a named propositional rule with its number of premises."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:57
msgid "`default_prop_prelude` returns the prelude used everywhere in QED: the constants `T`, `F`, `imp`, `not`, `and`, `or`, and the rules `imp_intro`, `imp_elim`, `and_intro`, `and_elim_l`, `and_elim_r`, `or_intro_l`, `or_intro_r`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:65
msgid "`install_prop_prelude` defines the six prelude constants in a kernel state through the `DefOK` gate."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:71
msgid "It is idempotent: a constant that already has exactly the canonical definition is kept. A constant with the same name that is only declared, or defined differently, is refused with `DefinitionAlreadyExists`, and one with another type with `ConstTypeConflict`. On an empty state it adds six `DefOK` certificates."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:73
msgid "`prop_prelude_rule_count`, `prop_prelude_rule_at` and `prop_prelude_has_rule`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:75
msgid "These functions read the rule list of a prelude."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:83
msgid "`prop_truth_name` and `prop_false_name`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:85
msgid "These functions return the names of the truth and falsity constants."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:110
msgid "Connective terms"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:112
msgid "`prop_mk_and`, `prop_mk_imp`, `prop_mk_not` and `prop_mk_or`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:114
msgid "These functions build the connective terms. They return the *basis* form, the right-hand side of the definition applied to the arguments, not an application of the constant."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:123
msgid "The forms are"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:134
msgid "where $t$ is the truth term $(\\lambda x.\\,x) = (\\lambda x.\\,x)$ and $\\bot_0$ is the falsity term of `logic_prop_false_term`. They fail with `NotBoolTerm` when an argument is not a proposition and with `TypeMismatch` when it is ill-typed. They do not need the prelude to be installed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:136
msgid "[!WARNING] The binder $f$ of the conjunction is a variable with the fixed name `_p_and` and type $\\mathit{bool} \\to \\mathit{bool} \\to \\mathit{bool}$, and the builders do not rename it. If an argument contains a free variable with exactly that name and type, the builder captures it and the result is not $p \\wedge q$; `logic_prop_and_intro_thm` and the rules built on it then fail. Do not use the name `_p_and` for your own variables."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:139
msgid "`prop_dest_and`, `prop_dest_imp`, `prop_dest_not` and `prop_dest_or`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:141
msgid "These functions recognise connective terms and return their arguments, or `None`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:150
msgid "They accept the basis form, and also an application of the prelude constant when the state holds the canonical definition theorem for it. A constant with the right name but without that definition is not recognised, so a look-alike constant cannot pass as a connective."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:152
msgid "`logic_prop_truth_term` and `logic_prop_false_term`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:154
msgid "These functions return the basis terms of truth and falsity: $(\\lambda x.\\,x) = (\\lambda x.\\,x)$ and $(\\lambda p.\\,p) = (\\lambda p.\\,t)$, the latter being $\\forall p.\\,p$ in HOL's encoding."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:180
msgid "Definition theorems"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:182
msgid "`logic_prop_def_imp`, `logic_prop_def_not`, `logic_prop_def_and` and `logic_prop_def_or`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:184
msgid "These functions return the definition theorem of a connective constant from the state, for example $\\vdash \\mathit{and} = \\lambda p\\,q.\\,p \\wedge q$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:193
msgid "They fail with `UnknownConst` when the prelude is not installed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:197
msgid "`logic_bool_false_def_thm` returns the definition theorem of `F`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:203
msgid "`logic_prop_def_lhs` and `logic_prop_def_rhs`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:205
msgid "These functions return the two sides of an equational theorem, typically a definition."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:214
msgid "`logic_prop_unfold_head(state, th_def, th)` replaces the head constant of the conclusion of `th` by its definition: from $\\vdash c = \\lambda \\bar x.\\,b$ and $\\Gamma \\vdash c\\,\\bar a$ it derives $\\Gamma \\vdash (\\lambda \\bar x.\\,b)\\,\\bar a$, with head β-redexes contracted by `logic_beta_normalize_eq`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:220
msgid "The head must be the defined constant, applied to at most two arguments; otherwise the function fails with `AlphaMismatch` or `TypeMismatch`. Redexes along the application spine are contracted. With one argument, $(\\lambda x.\\,b)\\,a$ is a head redex and the result is $b[a/x]$; with two arguments the head is $((\\lambda x\\,y.\\,b)\\,a_1)\\,a_2$, whose function part $(\\lambda x\\,y.\\,b)\\,a_1$ is contracted first to $\\lambda y.\\,b[a_1/x]$ and then the head redex, so the conclusion is the basis form $b[a_1/x][a_2/y]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:222
msgid "`logic_prop_unfold_imp`, `logic_prop_unfold_not`, `logic_prop_unfold_and` and `logic_prop_unfold_or`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:224
msgid ""
"These functions are `logic_prop_unfold_head` with the definition of one connective constant. `logic_prop_unfold_not` turns $\\Gamma \\vdash \\mathit{not}\\,p$ into $\\Gamma \\vdash \\n"
"eg p$ in basis form, and the two-argument forms return the basis form of the connective as well."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:253
msgid "Kernel rule wrappers"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:255
msgid "These functions call the corresponding kernel rule unchanged. They exist so that upper layers depend on `logic` rather than reaching into the kernel for every step."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:257
msgid "`logic_refl`, `logic_assume`, `logic_add_assum` and `logic_trans`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:266
msgid "They are `refl_checked`, `assume_checked`, `add_assum_checked` and `trans_checked`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:268
msgid "`logic_mk_comb_rule`, `logic_abs_rule` and `logic_beta_rule`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:276
msgid "They are `mk_comb_rule_checked`, `abs_rule_checked` and `beta_rule_checked`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:278
msgid "`logic_eq_mp` and `logic_deduct_antisym`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:285
msgid "They are `eq_mp_checked` and `deduct_antisym_rule_checked`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:287
msgid "`logic_inst_type` and `logic_inst`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:294
msgid "They are `inst_type` and `inst_checked`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:296
msgid "Extension wrappers"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:298
msgid "These functions call the kernel extension gates and audit readers unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:300
msgid "`logic_register_typedef` and `logic_typedef_contract`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:307
msgid "They are `ks_register_type_definition` (`TypeDefOK`) and `ks_typedef_contract`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:315
msgid "It is `ks_specify_const` (`SpecOK`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:317
msgid "`logic_register_ind_infinity_anchor` and `logic_ind_infinity_anchor`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:324
msgid "They are `ks_register_ind_infinity_axiom` and `ks_ind_infinity_axiom`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:326
msgid "`logic_extension_cert_count`, `logic_extension_cert_at` and `logic_conservative_replay_ok`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:334
msgid "They are `ks_extension_cert_count`, `ks_extension_cert_at` and `ks_conservative_replay_ok`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:336
msgid "Equality tools"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:340
msgid "`logic_eq_sym` derives symmetry of equality: from $\\Gamma \\vdash s = t$ it proves $\\Gamma \\vdash t = s$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:346
msgid "The derivation uses REFL, MK_COMB twice and EQ_MP; the [kernel tutorial](../tutorial/kernel.md) builds the same rule step by step. Fails with `NotAnEquality` when the premise is not an equation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:350
msgid "`logic_eq_mp_bool` is EQ_MP with an explicit check that the second premise is a proposition."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:356
msgid "`logic_apply_fun_eq` and `logic_apply_fun_eq2`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:358
msgid "These functions apply both sides of a function equation to arguments: from $\\Gamma \\vdash f = g$ they prove $\\Gamma \\vdash f\\,a = g\\,a$, or $\\Gamma \\vdash f\\,a\\,b = g\\,a\\,b$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:365
msgid "`logic_beta_normalize_eq` and `logic_normalize_prop_beta`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:367
msgid "`logic_beta_normalize_eq` contracts the β-redexes along the application spine of the right-hand side of an equation (the head redex, or a redex in the function part of an application) for as long as there is one: from $\\Gamma \\vdash s = t$ it proves $\\Gamma \\vdash s = t'$. `logic_normalize_prop_beta` reduces the conclusion of a theorem more thoroughly: it repeatedly contracts the leftmost-outermost redex found along application spines (not under abstractions), up to 512 steps, and from $\\Gamma \\vdash c$ proves $\\Gamma \\vdash c'$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:374
msgid "Both chain kernel steps (BETA and TRANS, plus REFL and MK_COMB to reach redexes inside applications), so the result is a kernel theorem, not a rewritten term. After 512 steps `logic_normalize_prop_beta` returns what it has reached, which may still contain redexes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:376
msgid "`logic_beta_nf_bool_term` and `logic_beta_nf_bool_term_deep`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:378
msgid "These functions compute the β-normal form of a proposition by proving $\\vdash t = t'$ and returning $t'$. The deep form repeats until a fixed point, within a bound."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:385
msgid "The tactics layer uses them to compare a finished theorem with its goal up to β-equivalence."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:387
msgid "Propositional theorems"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:389
msgid "These functions derive the natural-deduction rules of propositional logic from the kernel rules and the connective definitions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:391
msgid "`logic_prop_truth_thm` and `logic_prop_truth_const_thm`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:393
msgid "`logic_prop_truth_thm` proves the truth term, $\\vdash (\\lambda x.\\,x) = (\\lambda x.\\,x)$, by REFL. `logic_prop_truth_const_thm` proves $\\vdash \\top$ for the constant `T`, using its definition."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:400
msgid "`logic_prop_and_intro_thm`, `logic_prop_and_elim_l_thm` and `logic_prop_and_elim_r_thm`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:402
msgid "These functions are conjunction introduction and elimination."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:418
msgid "`logic_prop_imp_intro_thm`, `logic_prop_imp_elim_thm` and `logic_prop_imp_refl`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:420
msgid "`logic_prop_imp_intro_thm(state, pre, p, th)` discharges the hypothesis `p`; `logic_prop_imp_elim_thm` is modus ponens; `logic_prop_imp_refl` proves $\\vdash p \\Rightarrow p$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:436
msgid "`logic_prop_imp_intro_thm` fails when `p` is not a hypothesis of `th`. `logic_prop_imp_elim_thm` fails when the second theorem does not prove the antecedent."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:438
msgid "`logic_prop_not_elim` and `logic_prop_ex_falso_thm`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:440
msgid "`logic_prop_not_elim` derives falsity from a proposition and its negation; `logic_prop_ex_falso_thm(state, pre, th_false, q)` derives any proposition `q` from falsity."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:453
msgid "`logic_prop_or_intro_l_thm` and `logic_prop_or_intro_r_thm`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:455
msgid "These functions are disjunction introduction: `logic_prop_or_intro_l_thm(state, pre, th_p, q)` proves $p \\vee q$ from $p$, and `logic_prop_or_intro_r_thm(state, pre, p, th_q)` proves $p \\vee q$ from $q$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:462
msgid ""
"There is no disjunction elimination: with $p \\vee q := \\n"
"eg p \\Rightarrow q$ it would need excluded middle, which the prelude does not derive. The [logic design](../design/logic.md) explains this."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:514
msgid "Replay helpers"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:516
msgid "The tactics layer closes goals by replaying kernel steps. These helpers make a theorem fit a goal sequent exactly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:520
msgid "`logic_prop_assume` is `logic_assume` under the name the replay code uses."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:528
msgid "`logic_prop_strengthen_to_hyps(state, hyps, th)` adds the missing hypotheses of `hyps` to `th` by weakening, and checks that the result has exactly the hypotheses `hyps`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:534
msgid "It fails when `th` has a hypothesis outside `hyps`: weakening can add hypotheses but never remove them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:536
msgid "`logic_prop_close_hypothesis` and `logic_prop_ensure_sequent`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:538
msgid "`logic_prop_close_hypothesis(state, hyps, c)` proves the sequent $\\mathit{hyps} \\vdash c$ when `c` is one of `hyps`. `logic_prop_ensure_sequent(state, hyps, c, th)` checks that `th` concludes `c` and returns it with exactly the hypotheses `hyps`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:547
msgid "`logic_prop_discharge_imp_prefix(state, pre, [a1, ..., an], th)` discharges the hypotheses $a_n, \\dots, a_1$ in turn, turning $\\Gamma \\vdash q$ into $\\Gamma \\setminus \\{a_1, \\dots, a_n\\} \\vdash a_1 \\Rightarrow \\dots \\Rightarrow a_n \\Rightarrow q$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:553
msgid "`logic_prop_replay_imp_elim_backward` and `logic_prop_replay_imp_elim_backward_thm`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:555
msgid "These functions finish a backward `apply` step. Given the goal hypotheses, an implication $a \\Rightarrow b$ (as a hypothesis term, or as a theorem) and a proof of $a$, they prove $b$ under exactly the goal hypotheses."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:562
msgid "`logic_prop_replay_and_elim_l` and `logic_prop_replay_and_elim_r`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:564
msgid "These functions eliminate a conjunction and check that the result is the expected term."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:573
msgid "`logic_prop_merge_conjunction(state, pre, th_a, th_b, and_term)` introduces a conjunction and checks that it is `and_term`; `split` uses it to join its two branches."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:579
msgid "`logic_prop_or_wrap_left` and `logic_prop_or_wrap_right`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:581
msgid "These functions introduce a disjunction from its left or right branch and check, up to β-normal form, that it is the expected disjunction; `left` and `right` use them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:588
msgid "The arguments are the full disjunction, the other branch and the proof of the chosen branch."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:611
msgid "Theorem catalog"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:613
msgid "The catalog is the single list of theorem names that proof scripts may use with `exact` and `apply`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:615
msgid "`PropTheoremClass` and `PropTheoremEntry`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:617
msgid "`PropTheoremEntry` describes a catalog name: its number of premises and how `exact` and `apply` may use it. `PropTheoremClass` says how a name closes a goal."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/logic.md:634
msgid "`DirectClose` closes a goal outright (`truth`, `imp_refl`, `eq_refl`). `ContextDerived` closes a goal using facts found among the hypotheses and locals (`and_elim_l` from a conjunction in context). `ImplicationBacked` turns a goal into the premise of an implication (`apply or_intro_l`). A `None` class means the name is not available in that mode."
msgstr ""

