msgid "`parse_theorem_script_raw` parses one theorem script. Steps may be on one line separated by `;`, or one per line. `parse_theorem_file_raw` parses a file of scripts separated by lines containing `qed`; a file with a single theorem may omit it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/parser.md:409
msgid "Binder types and goals are parsed but not resolved; the prover lowers them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/parser.md:429
msgid "Definitions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/parser.md:433
msgid "`parse_def_function(state, env, src)` parses a definition `def <name>(<x> : <type>, ...) : <type> { <body> }` and admits it through the kernel's `DefOK` gate as the constant $\\mathit{name} = \\lambda x \\dots.\\,\\mathit{body}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/parser.md:439
msgid "It returns the extended state and the definition theorem. Kernel refusals (a free variable in the body, a name already defined) come back as `Sig` errors. This utility is not part of theorem-script syntax."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:1
msgid "prover API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:5
msgid "The `prover` package (`Luna-Flow/QED/prover`) runs theorem scripts. It parses a script or a file of scripts, installs the propositional prelude if asked, lowers each goal, runs the steps with the `tactics` package (scheduling `{ ... }` branch blocks itself), and returns either a kernel theorem, a structured failure, or an unfinished-proof report for a script with a `hole`. It also publishes the regression corpus that ties the [user manual](../manual.md) to the tests."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:7
msgid "The package orchestrates and reports; it holds no logical authority. The [prover design](../design/prover.md) explains the result model, and the [prover tutorial](../tutorial/prover.md) runs scripts from MoonBit."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:20
msgid "The examples on this page are blackbox tests. They refer to this package as `@prover` and also use `@kernel`, so they import both packages."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:24
msgid "`ProverOptions`, `default_prover_options` and `prover_options`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:26
msgid "`ProverOptions` has one switch: whether to install the propositional prelude into the state before running. `default_prover_options()` turns it on; `prover_options(b)` sets it explicitly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:37
msgid "Without the prelude, `T`, `F` and the catalog theorems are unavailable unless the state already defines them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:39
msgid "Running scripts"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:43
msgid "`prove_theorem_script_detailed(state, src, opts)` runs one theorem script and returns a three-way result."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:49
msgid "Header binders `(x : A)` become locals of the goal. The steps run in order; a step followed by branch blocks runs, and then each block runs on its subgoal in its own frame. The first failing step stops the script. A `hole` stops it as unfinished. If all steps succeed but goals remain, the result is a failure with `UnsolvedGoals`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:51
msgid "`prove_theorem_script` and `prove_theorem_script_with_diagnostics`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:53
msgid "`prove_theorem_script` runs one script and returns the theorem and the state, or the failure or unfinished report as an error. `prove_theorem_script_with_diagnostics` is a deprecated alias of it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:60
msgid "The two functions behave identically; both carry the full diagnostics in `ProverScriptError`, so new code should call `prove_theorem_script`, and `prove_theorem_script_with_diagnostics` is marked `#deprecated` and will be removed in a later release. The returned state is the state the proof ran in, with the prelude installed when requested; proved theorems are not added to it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:64
msgid "`prove_theorem_file_results_detailed(state, src, opts)` runs every theorem of a file whose theorems are separated by `qed` lines, and returns one result per theorem."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:70
msgid "A failing or unfinished theorem does not stop the file: later theorems are still checked. The whole file fails (`FileFailed`) only when it cannot be parsed or the prelude cannot be installed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:74
msgid "`prove_theorem_file_detailed` runs a file and summarises it as one result: the first failure if any, else the first unfinished theorem if any, else the last success."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:84
msgid "`ProverRunResult` is the outcome of one script."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:96
msgid "`ProverSuccess` carries the theorem, the name of the script and the state it was proved in."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:108
msgid "`ProverFailure` describes where and why a script failed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:128
msgid "`kind` and `error` say which layer failed; `detail` renders the error. The position fields locate the failure in the source: `raw_offset` for parse errors, the goal span for lowering errors, and the step index, branch path, step span and step text for tactic errors, together with the goal and locals at that point. Fields that do not apply are `None` or empty."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:132
msgid "`ProverUnfinished` reports a script that reached a `hole`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:151
msgid "It has the same position fields as a failure, the goal the hole stands for (always present) and the hole's name if it has one. It is not a theorem."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:153
msgid "`ProverLocalHyp` and `prover_local_hyp`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:155
msgid "`ProverLocalHyp` is a named local hypothesis in a report; `prover_local_hyp` builds one."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:168
msgid "`prover_unfinished` builds an `Unfinished` result from its fields, in the order theorem name, goal source, goal span, step index, branch path, step span, step source, current goal, locals, hole name, detail and raw offset."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:174
msgid "It exists for tests and renderers that need a fixed unfinished report."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:176
msgid "`ProverError` and `ProverDiagnosticKind`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:178
msgid "`ProverError` wraps the error of the layer that failed; `ProverDiagnosticKind` names the layer."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:200
msgid "`ProverScriptError` is the error side of `prove_theorem_script`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:209
msgid "`ProverFileRunResult`, `ProverFileReport` and `ProverFileItemResult`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:211
msgid "A file run either fails as a whole or yields one item per theorem, in order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:284
msgid "The file is `examples/multi_with_hole.qed`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:286
msgid "Regression corpus"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:288
msgid "The corpus is the single list of scripts that the tests run and that the documentation may quote. Each case has an identifier, a script, the layout of its steps, and the capability or failure it demonstrates."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:290
msgid "`positive_corpus_cases`, `negative_corpus_cases`, `unfinished_corpus_cases` and `quantifier_corpus_cases`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:292
msgid "These functions return the cases of each kind."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:303
msgid "A positive case must prove `expected_goal` after declaring the boolean constants `const_names`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:319
msgid "A negative case must fail with the error shape `expected_error`. The constants are declared as booleans, unary or binary boolean functions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:336
msgid "An unfinished case must stop at the given step, branch path and hole, with the given detail."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:352
msgid "`QuantifierCorpusCase` and `QuantifierSurfaceOutcome`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:354
msgid "A quantifier case covers the binder and raw `forall` surface and may succeed, fail or be unfinished."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:378
msgid "`ScriptLayout`, `PositiveCatalogClass`, `PositiveTacticCapability`, `NegativeFailureClass` and `NegativeErrorShape`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:380
msgid "These enums classify cases; the label functions below render them as the stable strings used in tests and documentation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:428
msgid "`script_layout_label`, `catalog_label`, `capability_label`, `negative_failure_class_label`, `negative_error_shape_label` and `quantifier_surface_outcome_label`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:430
msgid "These functions return the stable snake-case label of a classification value, such as `inline_sequential`, `local_fact` or `intro_exact`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:441
msgid "Mapping matrix"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:443
msgid "The mapping matrix links each corpus case to the documentation anchor where it may appear, such as `manual:runnable_examples`, and says whether it is a public example."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:445
msgid "`MappingMatrixEntry` and `mapping_matrix_entries`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:461
msgid "`MappingCaseKind`, `mapping_case_positive`, `mapping_case_negative` and `mapping_case_unfinished`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:463
msgid "`MappingCaseKind` says whether a case is positive, negative or unfinished; the three functions return its constructors."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:477
msgid "`MappingDocVisibility` and its constructor functions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/prover.md:479
msgid "`MappingDocVisibility` says whether a case may be quoted in the documentation as an example, as a failure example, or not at all. `mapping_doc_visibility_public_unfinished_example` returns `PublicExample`: unfinished examples are public examples."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:1
msgid "research_rewrite API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:5
msgid "The `research_rewrite` package (`Luna-Flow/QED/research_rewrite`) is a research prototype for rewriting and simplifying proof goals. It rewrites the conclusion of a goal at a chosen position, records how each step can be replayed with kernel rules, and can check such a record again. It depends only on `kernel` and `logic`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:7
msgid "[!IMPORTANT] This package is research-only and not shipped. No other package uses it, theorem scripts have no `rewrite` or `simp` step, and the go/no-go review in `research/rewrite-simplify/` concluded \"No-Go\" for promotion. Its interface may change or disappear."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:10
msgid "The [research_rewrite design](../design/research_rewrite.md) explains the replay model, and the [research_rewrite tutorial](../tutorial/research_rewrite.md) runs a rewrite."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:24
msgid "The examples on this page are blackbox tests. They refer to this package as `@research_rewrite` and also use `@kernel` and `@logic`, so they import all of these packages."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:26
msgid "Requests"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:28
msgid "`RewriteWitness`, `ConnectorKind` and their constructor functions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:30
msgid "`RewriteWitness` says which equation justifies a rewrite: a local hypothesis of the goal by name, an explicit theorem, the definition of a connective, or β-normalisation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:56
msgid "A local or explicit witness must conclude an equation $l = r$. A connective unfold rewrites an application of the prelude constant to its β-normalised definition."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:58
msgid "`RewriteDirection`, `research_left_to_right` and `research_right_to_left`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:60
msgid "`RewriteDirection` says whether to replace $l$ by $r$ or $r$ by $l$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:72
msgid "Right-to-left is supported for local and explicit witnesses only; folding a definition or β-expanding fails honestly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:74
msgid "`RewriteSiteStep` and its constructor functions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:76
msgid "A site is a path from the root of the conclusion to the subterm to rewrite: into the function or the argument of an application, or into the body of an abstraction."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:90
msgid "The empty path is the whole conclusion. Rewriting under an abstraction (`AbsBody`) is not supported and fails honestly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:92
msgid "`RewriteRequest` and `research_rewrite_request`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:94
msgid "A request combines a witness, a direction and a site."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:106
msgid "Rewriting"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:110
msgid "`research_rewrite_term_concl(state, prelude, locals, concl, request)` performs one rewrite of the proposition `concl`. `locals` are the named hypotheses that `LocalEquality` may refer to."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:116
msgid "It returns the rewritten conclusion with a replay obligation of one segment, or an honest failure: the conclusion is not a proposition, the site does not exist, the witness is unknown or does not match the subterm at the site, or a kernel step fails."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:118
msgid "`research_simplify_term_concl` and `SimplifyConfig`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:120
msgid "`research_simplify_term_concl` rewrites repeatedly until nothing changes: in each round it unfolds a connective if `allow_unfold`, β-normalises if `allow_beta`, and applies each request of `rewrite_requests` that matches. `step_limit` bounds the number of segments."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:134
msgid "It returns `NoChange` when no step applies, and fails honestly with \"step limit exceeded\" when more than `step_limit` segments would be needed, or with \"step limit must be non-negative\"."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:138
msgid "`RewriteResult` is the outcome of a rewrite or simplification."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:148
msgid "The result is a term and a record, not a theorem: nothing here proves the rewritten goal."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:150
msgid "Replay obligations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:152
msgid "`ReplayStepKind` and `research_step_name`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:154
msgid "`ReplayStepKind` names the kernel-level steps a segment needs: resolving the witness, symmetry, congruence (one per site step), unfolding a definition, β-normalisation. `research_step_name` returns the constructor name as a string."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:168
msgid "`ReplaySegment`, `ReplayObligation` and their constructor functions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:170
msgid "A segment records one rewrite: the request, the conclusion before and after, and its steps. An obligation chains segments from a first conclusion to a last."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:192
msgid "`research_validate_replay_obligation(state, prelude, locals, obligation)` checks an obligation by rebuilding every segment from its request with kernel rules and comparing the result, the steps and the chaining with what the obligation records."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/research_rewrite.md:198
msgid "A hand-made or stale obligation fails with a `LogicError`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/tactics.md:1
msgid "tactics API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/tactics.md:5
msgid "The `tactics` package (`Luna-Flow/QED/tactics`) runs proofs backwards. A `ProofState` holds a root goal and a list of pending subgoals; each `TacticStep` transforms the first pending goal, and when the last goal closes the package replays the recorded steps forward through the `logic` and kernel rules to build the theorem. `ps_qed` returns that theorem only if it proves exactly the root goal. The package depends on `kernel` and `logic`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/tactics.md:7
msgid "The relation between steps and kernel rules is explained in the [tactics design](../design/tactics.md); the [tactics tutorial](../tutorial/tactics.md) proves goals step by step. Users who write proof scripts reach this package through the [prover](prover.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/tactics.md:22
msgid "The examples on this page are blackbox tests. They refer to this package as `@tactics` and also use `@kernel`, `@logic` and `@parser`, so they import all of these packages."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/tactics.md:28
msgid "`Goal` is a sequent to prove: hypotheses and a conclusion, all propositions."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/tactics.md:37
msgid "Connectives in a goal are the basis terms of the `logic` builders, as produced by the parser."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/tactics.md:39
msgid "`mk_goal`, `goal_hyps`, `goal_concl` and `goal_hyp_count`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/tactics.md:41
msgid "These functions build a goal and read it; `goal_hyps` returns a copy."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/tactics.md:50
msgid "Steps"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/tactics.md:54
msgid "`TacticStep` is one backward proof step."
msgstr ""

