#: manual/conformance.md:203
msgid "Checklist"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance.md:205
msgid "[ ] `HOL-TYPES-001` [required; critical] HOL types, type constructors, non-empty carriers, and type substitution are well-formed. (open)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance.md:206
msgid "[ ] `HOL-TERMS-001` [required; critical] Typed terms, alpha-equivalence, capture-avoiding substitution, beta evaluation, and type instantiation preserve typing and meaning. (open)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance.md:207
msgid "[ ] `HOL-RULES-001` [required; critical] Every primitive theorem rule preserves semantic validity under its stated side conditions. (open)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance.md:208
msgid "[ ] `HOL-EXT-001` [required; critical] Definition, specification, and type-definition extensions have their stated local semantic laws and preserve validity of old-language sequents. (open)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance.md:209
msgid "[ ] `HOL-EXT-002` [required; high] History-indexed derivations require extension witnesses at the preceding history and preserve old-language validity across supported histories. (open)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance.md:210
msgid "[ ] `HOL-ERASE-001` [required; high] A derivation at a history that begins with a definition, a specification or a type definition yields a derivation at the preceding history in which every occurrence of the declared constant is replaced by its instantiated definition, by the choice term of its instantiated predicate, or (for a type definition) in which the defined type is translated to its representation type; sequents not mentioning the declared symbols are derivable unchanged. (open)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance.md:211
msgid "[ ] `HOL-GROW-001` [required; high] A finite declaration table records fresh constant allocation and preserves compatibility with the fixed total signature; old declarations remain available after a fresh allocation. (open)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance.md:212
msgid "[ ] `HOL-INFINITY-001` [required; high] The executable infinity registration is an audit marker; the canonical object-language infinity sentence is established by the Lean rule, not by the MoonBit gate. (open)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance.md:213
msgid "[ ] `KERNEL-BOUNDARY-001` [required; critical] Only checked kernel constructors and extension gates can produce theorem objects; frontend code cannot forge them. (open)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance.md:214
msgid "[ ] `KERNEL-PROVENANCE-001` [required; critical] A theorem is admissible only in a compatible declaration lineage; divergent sibling histories cannot exchange theorem evidence. (open)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance.md:215
msgid "[ ] `KERNEL-NAMES-001` [required; critical] Checked rules that compare constants by name rather than by identity stamp are sound: a theorem admissible in a state resolves each constant name it mentions to the identity it carries, so within one state equal names mean equal constants. (open)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance.md:216
msgid "[ ] `FRONTEND-LOWERING-001` [required; high] Parsing and elaboration preserve the proposition and resolved constant identity supplied to the kernel, or fail closed. (open)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conformance.md:217
msgid "[ ] `REFINEMENT-001` [required; critical] MoonBit types, terms, theorem objects, state transitions, and theorem constructors are connected to the Typed HOL calculus by a checked refinement or proof-producing translation. (open)"
msgstr ""

